  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Give the full forms of CT and MRI. How are they different from each other? (3 marks)', 'CT stands for Computed (Computerised) Tomography; MRI stands for Magnetic Resonance Imaging. Differences: (1) CT uses X-rays (ionising radiation) taken from many angles around the body and combined by a computer into cross-sectional images, whereas MRI uses a powerful magnetic field and radio-frequency waves acting on hydrogen nuclei, with no ionising radiation. (2) CT is quick and best for bones, bleeding, lungs and emergency trauma; MRI gives much better contrast of soft tissues such as the brain, spinal cord, ligaments and muscles. (3) MRI takes longer, is noisy and costly, and cannot be used with some metal implants or pacemakers, while CT exposes the patient to radiation.', null, '[{"point": "CT - Computed Tomography; MRI - Magnetic Resonance Imaging", "marks": 1}, {"point": "CT uses X-rays (ionising radiation) rotated around the body; MRI uses a strong magnetic field and radio waves (no ionising radiation)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "CT is faster, best for bones, bleeding, lungs and emergencies; MRI gives better soft-tissue contrast (brain, spinal cord, joints, muscles), takes longer, unsafe with some metal implants", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'bio-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
